{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgBzb3Sp1XUaMqkGZis5n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The O3 framework uses `getAsyncLifecycle`. You pass it a dynamic `import()` and it returns a function. That function does nothing until single-spa calls it to bootstrap or mount the extension or page. Only then does the module load, and the component is wrapped with `getLifecycle`.\n\n**How it works**\n\n1. **Declaration:** `getAsyncLifecycle(lazy, options)` returns `() => lazy().then(({ default: Component }) => getLifecycle(Component, options))`. The `import()` runs only when that returned function is called, so the component's bundle is deferred ([getLifecycle.ts:52-57](openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts)).\n2. **Wrapping:** `getLifecycle` passes the component through `openmrsComponentDecorator` (error boundaries, config, extension support) and then through `singleSpaReact` ([getLifecycle.ts:26-32](openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts)).\n3. **Route/extension loading:** `loadLifeCycles` in the routes package dynamically imports the app module by name, calls its `startupApp()` once, and then calls the named export (the function created by `getAsyncLifecycle`) ([load-lifecycles.ts:35-58](openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts), [load-lifecycles.ts:86-109](openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts)).\n\n**Usage in apps:** an app's `index.ts` exports the lifecycle, for example `export const root = getAsyncLifecycle(() => import('./root.component'), options);` ([esm-help-menu-app/src/index.ts:13](openmrs-esm-core/packages/apps/esm-help-menu-app/src/index.ts), [esm-devtools-app/src/index.ts:10](openmrs-esm-core/packages/apps/esm-devtools-app/src/index.ts)).\n\n**Documentation:** `packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getAsyncLifecycle.md` describes it as lazy loading via dynamic import \"only when it's needed,\" and recommends it for most modules. It is linked from `packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/API.md:129`. `getSyncLifecycle` is the non-lazy counterpart. `getAsyncExtensionLifecycle` is a deprecated alias.\n\nI did not check the patient-chart repo or the routes.json resolution path. Those would only add usage examples, not change the mechanism.\n\n**Declarations (best first)**\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 52, \"endLine\": 57},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 26, \"endLine\": 32},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 77, \"endLine\": 79},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 84, \"endLine\": 84},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts\", \"startLine\": 35, \"endLine\": 58},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts\", \"startLine\": 86, \"endLine\": 109},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/apps/esm-help-menu-app/src/index.ts\", \"startLine\": 13, \"endLine\": 13},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/apps/esm-devtools-app/src/index.ts\", \"startLine\": 10, \"endLine\": 10},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getAsyncLifecycle.md\", \"startLine\": 9, \"endLine\": 12},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/API.md\", \"startLine\": 129, \"endLine\": 129}\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1057,"cache_read_input_tokens":20241,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1057},"output_